 "requested_at": "2026-11-12T19:52:04.283498Z",
 "text": "The clause is enforceable. It mirrors the statutory interest obligation rather than departing from it.\n\nSection 107 of the *Residential Tenancies Act, 2006* requires a landlord to pay the tenant annual interest on a rent deposit at the rate set by the rent increase guideline for that year. The clause matches that rule: the landlord pays annually, and the rate is the guideline in effect when payment falls due. Because the clause gives the tenant no less than the Act requires, it does not conflict with section 7, which voids any term that waives or contracts out of the Act's protections. If a term offered less interest than section 107 requires, section 7 would override it, but this clause does not do that.\n\nI have not checked the current text of section 107 or the guideline-rate regulation against a live source, so confirm the exact wording and the timing rule before relying on this.\n\nVerdict: enforceable\nSection: 107",
